A||||18||FIEROCIOUS||Cam choices?||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||12:56 AM||||I searched for Camshafts on the seach engine and to be quite honest theres not much info!<P>So, can all those who are useing cams other than stock give me some info?<P>1. Cam manufacturer name and P/N.<BR>2. Intake lift.<BR>3. Exhaust lift.<BR>4. Intake duration. @.050<BR>5. Exhaust duration @ .050<BR>6. Intake centerline.<BR>7. Personal opinion(Idle, part throttle, WOT)<P>For those who realy know their stuff,<BR>8. Itake valve opening degrees.<BR>9. Intake closeing degrees.<BR>10. Exhaust opening degrees.<BR>11. Exhaust closeing degrees.<BR>12. Lobe seperation angle.<P>Has anyone tried a Lunati cam?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||05-08-2000||04:24 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.rconcepts.com/beard/dragnet/vendors/cams.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rconcepts.com/beard/dragnet/vendors/cams.html</A> <P>Here's a list of camshaft manufacturer's.  I agree there's not a whole lot of choice for the 60 degree V6 engines.  A Camaro owners swears by the Wolverine cams for the 2.8L.  Wolverine (Blue Racer) is now owned by Crane.  <A HREF="http://st23.yahoo.com/lib/cranecams/Chevycams.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://st23.yahoo.com/lib/cranecams/Chevycams.htm</A> <P><BR>GM, Crane, Lunati, Crower, Camcraft, Accelerated Motion, Comp Cams, ISKY are some of the more common makers.<P>Some websites below:  <P><A HREF="http://www.camcraftcams.com/catalogs/performance/gm.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.camcraftcams.com/catalogs/performance/gm.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.crower.com/19.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crower.com/19.pdf</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.amotion.com/amcams.html#196" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amotion.com/amcams.html#19661</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.engineparts.com/tech/chevpcs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.engineparts.com/tech/chevpcs .htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.competitioncams.com/speccard/16-232-4.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.competitioncams.com/speccard/16-232-4.txt</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com/master/apps/chevy1." TARGET=_blank>http://www.cranecams.com/master/apps/chevy1.</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.iskycams.com/CGI/Search_1704?company_type=PRODS&category=54&opt=id&mnu=1" TARGET=_blank>]http://www.iskycams.com/CGI/Search_1704?company_type=PRODS&category=54&opt=id&mnu=1]</A>         <BR> <A HREF="http://www.camcraftcams.com/camshafttuning.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.camcraftcams.com/camshafttuning.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.jvanc.com/epdmi/Line/wolverine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jvanc.com/epdmi/Line/wolverine.htm</A> <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||05-08-2000||01:00 PM||||I have a Wolverine Blue Racer WG-5061 in my 86SE V6.  It is a range "A" cam (+1 over stock) and I cannot tell too much of a difference in performance.  Although, anyone that has been around cars, automatically says "you have a cam in that don't you" when they hear it idle.  <P>The link that fierosound gave:<BR>  <A HREF="http://st23.yahoo.com/lib/cranecams/Chevycams.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://st23.yahoo.com/lib/cranecams/Chevycams.htm</A>  <P>has the specs for at least two of the Wolverine cams for the 2.8L V6.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||05-11-2000||02:34 AM||||mshill, how long have you had the cam? have you been satisfied with it?<P>Has anyone tried Crowers 03043 cam in their Fiero?<P>Terryk, how much duration or lift will the computer take without freaking out?||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||05-11-2000||02:39 AM||||Fierosound, Wolverine WG 1062 is the same cam as Cranes 254122 for computer controlled engines.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||05-11-2000||01:12 PM||||Fierocious,  I have only had the cam in since  mid-February and only have about 500 miles on it.  Like I said, acceleration wise it's hard to tell because I just rebuilt a tired V6 that had 189K miles on it so pretty much everything is new.  The idle quality is just a little rougher than stock and the sound (sans cat convertor is really excellent).<P>I think on my 86GT (95K miles) I am going to just try some 1.6:1 ratio roller rockers and see what the gets me. Since I don't want to tear the entire engine apart it will just be easier.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||05-11-2000||09:41 PM||||If you are going to keep your 2.8 a 2.8 than don’t get a cam that is too big like GM's performance cam that they use in the 3.4 crate engine (204 intake and 216 exhaust duration @ .050). That cam is designed for the 3.4 engine, which is larger in displacement. You will lose your good low-end torque in favor for high-end horsepower. In a car that weights 2700 pounds torque is important especially low-end torque. I would recommend the Crane Comucam 2020 with 198 intake and 204-exhaust duration @ .050 inch lift. I have run all the numbers of most of those cams on my Desktop dyno simulation program and the Crane Compucam 2020 will give you low end torque like the stock cam with great top end as well. I got 190-ft lb. of torque @ 3500 RPM and 171 HP @ 5500 RPM with ported heads and high performance manifolds on my dyno simulation program. <P>If you want me to give you any information on those cams I have most of it and can e-mail it to you. I can also give you the full graph of HP and torque figures with any combination of heads, exhaust type like headers or high performance manifolds, compression ratios, 1.5 or 1.6 rockers, nitrous, blowers, turbo chargers, roller cams, carburetors Vs fuel injection, and even what an overbore would give you. You name it and I can probably run a very accurate simulation for you. Just e-mail me and I will help you out the best I can.   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Taijiguy||05-12-2000||12:16 AM||||Hey 88,<P>I got that program too...you should see the specs for my 2.8 to 3.4 build- 380 horses @ 8000 rpm with torque peaking just shy of 300 lbs/ft at 6000 rpm. Can't wait to start building!||||reg||12
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||05-12-2000||12:22 PM||||Yeah, I Have the program also, the Crane Compucam shows the highest numbers on my Desktop Dyno 2000, but on my Desktop Dyno 2.3 program Eddlebrock's cam has slightly more across the RPM band. Every cam made for the 2.8 is saved in cam. files. so if any of you all need info feel free to ask.<P>2.8<BR>Fully ported heads <P>stainless steel valves<BR>back cut 30 degrees under cut valve stem.<P>Crane cams valve springs<P>Ported intake manifold<P>Fiero store headers<P>Custom exhaust no cat<P>forged pistons 9.5:1<P>Accel 300+ ignition and coil<P>3.1 fuel injectors<P>adj. fuel pressure regulator @ 47psi<P>Thats all I can think of for now.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||05-12-2000||03:03 PM||||What does the big GM performance cam do on the desktop dyno.  I don't know all the specs, it's not the truck cam.  There are two cams made for the 60 degree engine with 427 intake and 454 exhaust.  One has less duration and it's standard in the 3.4 crate motor.  The other has more, I don't know how much, and that's the one I have on my 3.1.  Now it's my theory that the reason my engine isn't running well (16.4 in quarter) is because the exhaust is plugged up.  The back pressure is so great that it blew the crap out of my y pipe which was already messed up.  By freeing up the exhaust flow, intake air flow should improve and the cam will be able to work like it should producing big upper rpm horsepower (right now there's only low rpm torquiness because it doesn't want to rev past 4000 rpm).  I have ported everything too (heads, intake and exhaust manifolds) non of this will do any good if there's no way for the engine to exhale.  Try running my 3.2 setup through your computer program with all the normal mods and 8.5:1 compression ratio (I think it's higher but I don't know for sure so use 8.5).  I appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||05-12-2000||03:08 PM||||Oh yeah, the O2 sensor is giving off a rich signal which further substantiates my lack of air theory  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||05-12-2000||07:03 PM||||Eric, I have sent the file to you in word format by e-mail so that you can open the document. I gave it to you in 7.0, 7.5, 8.5, and 9.0 compression ratios. If you want any other changes I can give them to you, just let me know by e-mail.||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||05-12-2000||07:12 PM||||Taijiguy, You can’t get 380 horsepower with that engine unless you have some kind of serious forced induction. Even if you could get that much power the engine would not last very long unless you spent thousands of dollars building the short block and it definitely would not work with the computer and the stock induction system. If you want a reliable 380 horsepower than get a v8. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||05-12-2000||08:12 PM||||Thanks for doing that for me.  Those numbers are impressive 190hp and 210ftlbs for the 9.0 compression version.  The torque curve kind of phases out at 4500 or so, but peak hp is around 5000-5500.  Right now my car doesn't even like to rev past 4000 rpm.  I'm guessing I have about 140 hp and 200ftlbs of torque at the flywheel.  I'm gonna try the vacuum test tommorow and see what it says.  I know the y pipe is bad and robbing power, but I'd still like to know how bad it really is.  Can the program do a hp gain for a new cat back exhaust with 2.5 inch mandrel pipes and a performance muffler like a flowmaster?||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||05-12-2000||08:31 PM||||If I remember right, there are two Crane CompuCams, one with a little higher lift for manual shift cars and one slightly milder one for auto tranny cars (so you can use the factory torque converter). I seem to remember when I ran those on my Desktop Dyno, that the hotter Crane and the GM HO cam were nearly equal. If anything, the GM cam improved flow a little more to the weaker exhaust ports by adding a little more lift, which Crane doesn't do.||||reg||1
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||05-13-2000||01:24 AM||||What are the specs on the GM cam?<P>Thanks all!||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||05-13-2000||09:56 AM||||I got these specs for the GM HO cam off the Sallee Chevrolet site.<P>Camshaft: Hydraulic with flat tappets<BR>Valve Lift (Intake/Exhaust): .426"/.454"<BR>Duration at .050" lift (Intake/Exhaust): 204/216||||reg||1
Z||000017||spiz||05-13-2000||10:38 AM||||gm cam part# 12364059 427/454 lift 204/216 duration 112 lobe center. this is the hot cam for the v6||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||05-13-2000||09:06 PM||||Eric, those dynos are with a high performance exhaust system (mufflers and high performance manifolds). I consider ported fiero manifolds high performance. I don't know the size of the pipe. The program assumes the typical exhaust system for that size engine. You would be better off adding a set of small diameter headers. I will send you the same dynos with Small tube headers and high performance mufflers. Borla sells a very nice cat back system made from 304 stainless with mandrel bends. You can get the Borla exhaust at Dason Auto Concepts Inc.<BR> 908 W. Hyde Park Blvd <BR>Inglewwod, CA 90302<P>Phone (310) 677-7185<BR>Fax (310) 677-2630 <BR>This is where I got mine. It was the last one on the shelf but he said he could order some more without any waiting.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-13-2000).]||||reg||1
